.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SAFE 1
.Os POSIX.1-2017
.Sh NAME
.Nm safe
.Nd digital safe for your secrets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bhkr
.Op Fl o
.Op Fl p Ar prompt
.Op Fl s Ar safe
.Op Oo Fl af Oc Ar secret
.Nm
.Fl F
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is a fork of Willy Goiffon's
.Xr safe 1
with a number of hardening changes; see
.Sx FORK NOTES
below.
.Pp
.Nm
stores secrets (files) encrypted on disk and lets you retrieve them,
given that you have the right password.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar secret
Decrypt file
.Ar secret
from your safe to stdout.
.It Fl a Ar secret
Encrypt stdin to your safe as
.Ar secret .
Use
.Fl f
to overwrite an existing secret.
.It Fl b
Batch mode.
Reads the master password from stdin, bypassing
.Ev SAFE_ASKPASS
even when no TTY is available.
.It Fl f
Force writing to
.Ar secret
if it exists.
Implies
.Fl a .
Also required to overwrite the
.Ar master
entry; see
.Sx MASTER PASSWORD .
.It Fl h
Print a quick usage text.
.It Fl k
Prompt for the password using an external program (see
.Ev SAFE_ASKPASS ) .
Ignored when
.Fl b
is set.
.It Fl p Ar prompt
Prompt for the password using text
.Ar prompt .
(default: "password:")
.It Fl r
Remember the password by pushing the derived key to a running agent.
.Ev SAFE_SOCK
must be set and point to the UNIX-domain socket bound by a running
.Xr safe-agent 1 .
.It Fl s Ar safe
Set the path to your safe as
.Ar safe .
Symbolic links are resolved with
.Xr realpath 3
before use.
(default: .secrets)
.It Fl F
Forget the key held by the running agent.
Connects to the agent socket named by
.Ev SAFE_SOCK ,
or, if it is unset, by
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/safe/sock ,
and sends the
.Sq F
command; see
.Xr safe-agent 1 .
The socket is validated as for any other use, except that root only
requires it to be a socket, and the agent's uid
.Pq Dv SO_PEERCRED
must match the socket's owner.
The agent accepts
.Sq F
from its own uid and from root, which is what makes
.Nm Fl F ,
run as root by the
.Ic safe-forget-all
script, work in the hardened profile.
If the socket does not exist or nothing listens on it, the command
succeeds.
.Fl F
cannot be combined with
.Fl a , Fl b , Fl f , Fl k , Fl o , Fl r ,
or with a secret argument.
.It Fl o
With
.Fl r ,
mark the pushed key as one-shot: the agent forgets it after the
next
.Sq D
or
.Sq E
request.
Requires
.Fl r .
.El
.Sh AGENT
When the agent is started,
.Nm
can use it to decrypt and encrypt entries rather than prompting for a password; the key never leaves the agent.
.Nm
will try to read the key from the agent whenever the
.Ev SAFE_SOCK
variable is set in the environment.
.Pp
To push the derived key to a running agent, use the
.Fl r
flag.
This must be done at least once after the agent starts so that the
agent has a key to hand out.
.Pp
If
.Ev SAFE_SOCK
is set but points to a socket that fails validation, a warning is
printed and the socket is ignored; the key is then derived from a
password as usual.
See
.Xr safe-agent 1
for the socket protocol and the parent-directory requirements.
.Sh MASTER PASSWORD
When you add your first secret to the safe, a
.Ar master
entry is created automatically.
This entry holds a constant payload, not your password, and is used to
check that you typed it correctly on subsequent calls and as the reference for the
key-derivation salt.
.Pp
Do not delete this entry as it could lead to a corrupted safe.
To recover from a lost or corrupted master entry, see
.Xr safe-store 5 .
.Pp
To overwrite the
.Ar master
entry in place,
.Fl f
is required.
The recovery form
.Bd -literal
  safe -f -a master < /dev/null
  password:
  verify:
.Ed
.Pp
re-encrypts the entry without first trying to decrypt it, which is what
you want when the current entry is broken.
The password prompt takes the existing master password; the bytes read
from stdin become the new master entry's payload and are never
interpreted, so any content works.
See
.Xr safe-store 5
for the full recovery procedure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Store a secret in your safe:
.Bd -literal
  $ safe -a secret/file < kitten.gif
.Ed
.Pp
List all secrets in
.Ev SAFE_DIR
(choose your weapon):
.Bd -literal
  $ tree --noreport $SAFE_DIR
  $ find $SAFE_DIR -type f
  $ ls -R $SAFE_DIR
  $ tar -C $SAFE_DIR -v -f /dev/null -c . | cut -d / -f 2-
.Ed
.Pp
Retrieve a secret from your safe:
.Bd -literal
  $ safe secret/file > kitten.gif
  password:
.Ed
.Pp
Use the agent so you only type the password once:
.Bd -literal
  $ eval $(safe-agent -t 900)
  $ safe -r
  password:
  $ safe secret/file > kitten.gif
.Ed
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "SAFE_SOCK"
.It Ev SAFE_DIR
Defines the location of your safe (default: .secrets)
.It Ev SAFE_SOCK
Path to the UNIX-domain socket used to communicate with the agent.
.It Ev SAFE_ASKPASS
If no TTY is available and
.Fl b
is not given, the program specified by this variable is used to read
the master password (default: ssh-askpass).
.It Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_MLOCK
If set, downgrade
.Xr mlock 2
failures from fatal errors to warnings.
.It Ev SAFE_ALLOW_NO_LANDLOCK
If set, run without Landlock when the kernel does not support it
(sandboxed Linux builds only).
Without it,
.Nm
refuses to run on such a kernel.
.El
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Without an agent, decryption streams to stdout, so a corrupt or
truncated secret may produce partial plaintext before the final MAC
check fails.
Check the exit status before consuming the output.
Through the agent, the entry is authenticated before any plaintext is
sent.
.Pp
In batch mode the master password is read from stdin.
Depending on how it was supplied, it may be visible to other processes
owned by the same user.
.Pp
The key is derived with Argon2id using libsodium's
.Dv MODERATE
parameters: 256 MiB of memory, 3 passes, one lane.
See
.Xr safe-store 5 .
.Sh FORK NOTES
This manual page describes the forked version of
.Nm .
User-visible differences from the upstream are summarized below; see
.Xr safe-store 5
for the on-disk format.
.Bl -bullet
.It
New entries are written in format 0x12: the header and the entry's
name are authenticated, and the final chunk is padded so that every
entry shorter than 256 bytes has the same size on disk.
Format 0x11 entries are still read.
See
.Xr safe-store 5 .
.It
Truncation and trailing data are rejected.
.It
The CREAM header is validated before it is used: magic, version,
chunk size and Argon2id parameters must match the frozen values
exactly.
.It
Secret writes are atomic: a temporary file in the same directory is
fsync'd and renamed into place.
.It
Secret paths are validated: no absolute paths, no "..", no "//", no
trailing "/", no leading "./".
.It
The safe directory and the agent socket are ownership- and
permission-checked.
.It
The agent wire protocol is framed and re-pushing a key while one is
loaded is reliable.
.It
The agent reports readiness to its parent before
.Ev SAFE_SOCK
is printed.
.It
.Fl b
reads the master password from stdin even with no TTY.
.It
.Fl f
is required to overwrite the
.Ar master
entry;
.Fl f Fl a Ar master
is the recovery form.
.It
.Fl F
forgets the key held by the running agent, with the agent's
.Sq F
command.
The agent accepts it from its own uid and from root, which is what
makes
.Fl F
usable in the hardened profile where the agent runs as a different
uid from the caller.
.It
.Fl o
marks the pushed key as one-shot: the agent forgets it after the
next
.Sq D
or
.Sq E .
.It
The agent never returns its key.
It decrypts
.Pq Sq D
and encrypts
.Pq Sq E
entries for its clients;
.Nm
uses both when an agent is available, and
.Xr safe-fill-host 1
uses
.Sq D .
The path is validated with the same rules
.Nm
uses and is authenticated as AEAD associated data.
.It
The agent accepts
.Fl u Ar uid
to admit a second uid for
.Sq D ;
.Sq P ,
.Sq I
and
.Sq E
remain euid-only, so
.Fl u
grants decrypt access only.
.It
The agent accepts
.Fl p Ar prefix
to restrict
.Sq D
and
.Sq E
to one subtree of the store.
.It
The agent writes
.Pa <socket_dir>/agent.pid
(0640) after binding the socket, for scripts and service managers.
.It
Peer identity is checked with
.Dv SO_PEERCRED ,
not only with Unix file permissions on the socket: the agent
distinguishes callers by uid, and
.Fl F
checks that the agent runs as the socket's owner.
.It
Memory holding keys and passwords is
.Xr mlock 2 Ns 'd ;
failures are fatal unless
.Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_MLOCK
is set.
.It
Linux release builds enable
.Xr seccomp 2
and
.Xr landlock 2
sandboxing and disable core dumps and
.Xr ptrace 2 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr safe-agent 1 ,
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ,
.Xr safe-fill-migrate 1 ,
.Xr safe-store 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Willy Goiffon Aq Mt dev@z3bra.org
.Pp
